<speak>
<prosody rate="105%">
<p>
Moderator: Welcome to this high-level panel at our Istanbul convening, navigating the complexities of weaving alliances from local action to collective impact. We are operating in an era marked by compounding crises, where the advancement of women's human rights faces the perilous ascent of exclusionary forces and a pervasive backlash against gender equality. The current political landscape is characterized by an alarming democratic decay, fostering fertile ground for opposition to human rights to flourish. To contextualize this, as of 2024, 45 countries had taken an authoritarian turn, effectively putting 72 percent of the global population under autocratic rule. Furthermore, our supplementary 2026 regional assessment observed a concerning 31 percent drop in independent judicial oversight across these exact jurisdictions, further compromising institutional accountability. We are witnessing deliberate, organized attempts to roll back established commitments, often executed through the insidious dismantling of policies or the hollowing out of international norms. To dissect the structural drivers propelling this policy backsliding, I turn first to Dr. Aris. How are anti-gender equality actors weaponizing this democratic erosion to reconfigure the policy landscape?
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Researcher: The foundational premise we must internalize is that the contemporary opposition to gender equality is a highly coordinated, transnational endeavor that exploits broader anxieties fueled by economic and social insecurity. Right-wing populist leaders and conservative networks are adeptly utilizing the malleable framing of gender ideology to portray the fundamental rights of women and LGBTIQ+ individuals as ideological colonization threatening the traditional family. By instilling moral panic, they position a regressive return to heteropatriarchy as the ultimate stabilization mechanism amidst systemic volatility. This is not merely rhetorical; it is heavily capitalized. According to comprehensive financial tracking, 54 organizations based primarily in the United States of America, the Russian Federation, and Europe provided over $700 million to anti-gender movements between 2009 and 2018. Looking at recent supplementary metrics from 2025, we noted that 55 percent of state-sponsored disinformation campaigns globally utilized this exact family-centric framing to delegitimize feminist advocacy. Anti-gender actors are structurally embedding themselves within mainstream politics, driving explicit policy backsliding that violates the core principle of non-retrogression in human rights frameworks.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Youth Activist: Dr. Aris rightly emphasizes the capitalization of these regressive movements, but we must urgently address how this highly subsidized anti-gender rhetoric is explicitly targeting and radicalizing younger generations. While youth have historically held politically liberal views, recent cross-country data reveals a profound and widening ideological gap. We are witnessing a staggering global gender divide; in countries as diverse as Germany, the Republic of Korea, and the United Kingdom, there is a gap of at least 25 percentage points between increasingly conservative young men and their more progressive female contemporaries. A revealing 2024 poll in the United Kingdom demonstrated that 18 percent of men aged 16 to 29 believe efforts to support women's equality have gone too far, which is more than twice the proportion of young women, who sit at just 8 percent. This is compounded by our internal 2025 data indicating that 47 percent of male respondents under the age of 25 in the Europe and Central Asia region consume algorithmic anti-feminist content on a weekly basis. Young women and girls championing bodily autonomy and comprehensive sexuality education are confronting a discursive environment systematically designed to circumscribe their fundamental freedoms.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Donor Representative: The demographic radicalization Samira highlights is deeply alarming, particularly when we juxtapose it with the systemic resourcing deficits paralyzing feminist counter-mobilization. The financial architecture supporting the human rights and gender equality architecture is currently vastly outmatched. As Dr. Aris noted regarding historical funding, the contemporary trajectory is even steeper. Data from the European Parliamentary Forum for Sexual and Reproductive Rights reveals that in Europe, annual anti-gender funding reached... excuse me, I misread my notes, the figure is $1.18 billion total over the 2018 to 2022 period, not annually. This represents a doubling of resources deployed specifically to finance strategic litigation, lobbying, and grassroots mobilization aimed at rolling back sexual and reproductive health and rights. Conversely, we project that by the end of 2026, core, unrestricted funding for intersectional feminist movements will experience an artificial 14 percent contraction due to increasingly stringent fiduciary compliance laws imposed by donor governments. Philanthropic entities and organizations like the Global Philanthropy Project, or GPP, recognize that bridging this funding gap requires a radical departure from rigid, project-based deliverables toward sustained, flexible support for autonomous civil society organizations.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
NGO Representative: Marcus, while acknowledging the funding disparity is necessary, we must confront the existential reality that even adequately funded organizations cannot operate when the state apparatus itself is actively dismantled. At the national level, policy backsliding is frequently initiated by undermining implementation mechanisms, rendering existing laws a dead letter. The systematic dismantling of national women's machineries has emerged as a low-cost strategy to expedite policy decay. During the 30-year review of the Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action, only half—specifically 52 percent—of States reported that their national women's machineries are provided with adequate financial resources and staff capacity to fulfil their crucial mandates. Furthermore, our independent 2025 organizational capacity assessment revealed that 41 percent of subnational budgets were quietly reallocated away from gender-based violence shelters. Curtailing autonomous civic space manifests through unnecessary administrative hurdles, barriers to registration, unwarranted raids, event cancellations by security forces, the weaponization of foreign agent designations, launching smear campaigns, criminalizing organizations, deploying judicial harassment, and resorting to physical violence. Grassroots organizations are bearing the brunt of this orchestrated paralysis.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Moderator: Elena provides a sobering account of the targeted dismantling of national mechanisms and the severe constriction of civic space. If domestic avenues for accountability are being systematically neutralized, how are these anti-gender coalitions operating within intergovernmental spaces? Dr. Aris, can you elaborate on the transnational dimension of this pushback?
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Researcher: Certainly. The opposition has expanded its repertoire beyond domestic borders, deploying sophisticated norm-spoiling tactics within specialized decision-making spaces like the Commission on the Status of Women and the Human Rights Council. These transnational coalitions of state and non-state actors attempt to water down existing human rights standards by revising agreed language, effectively challenging the normative framework from within. For instance, during the fifty-sixth session of the United Nations Human Rights Council in 2024, Member States tabled 30 amendments. Out of those, 12 amendments—representing 40 percent—explicitly targeted resolutions with a gender focus, proposing the deletion of established references to bodily autonomy, reproductive rights, and women and girl human right defenders. While 11 were ultimately rejected due to robust defense by progressive Member States, these norm-spoiling tactics successfully stall negotiations and divert immense energy toward defending existing language rather than advancing new protections. Furthermore, by 2026, we tracked a 22 percent decrease in the successful adoption of progressive gender policies at the municipal level, proving that multilateral gridlock effectively trickles down to paralyze local legislative action.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Youth Activist: The institutional gridlock Dr. Aris describes is precisely why my generation is forced to rely on decentralized, digital mobilization. However, the digital sphere, rather than serving as a sanctuary for democratic deliberation, has been weaponized into a primary theater for technology-facilitated gender-based violence. The United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization, UNESCO, reported that an astounding 73 percent of 901 women journalists interviewed experienced online violence. Moving beyond journalism into localized activism, research across 15 Latin American and Caribbean countries revealed that 80 percent of journalists and activists limited their participation in online networks due to this targeted harassment. Concurrently, an identical 80 percent feared for their actual physical safety or their life. Adding to this psychological toll, a recent 2026 survey among youth human rights defenders indicated that 38 percent of grassroots leaders report severe clinical burnout directly linked to digital surveillance. We cannot separate the defense of the rules-based international order from the urgent necessity to codify robust digital rights and protections for the women human rights defenders operating on the frontlines of this algorithmic warfare.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Audience Member 1: Thank you to the panel for this incisive analysis. My question is directed to Marcus and Elena. Given the severe constriction of civic space and the aggressive defunding mechanisms you both outlined, how can international donor institutions realistically bypass hostile state apparatuses to deliver vital resources directly to the grassroots, especially when bilateral official development assistance is inherently tied to state-to-state agreements?
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Donor Representative: That is the central paradox confronting the contemporary development architecture. When illiberal populism captures state institutions, traditional bilateral aid modalities often inadvertently legitimize the very actors dismantling gender mainstreaming. To counter this, progressive donors within the international community are attempting to pivot toward flexible, core-funding models channeled through independent, transnational intermediary funds. The Association for Women's Rights in Development, AWID, has consistently advocated for this paradigm shift. However, the primary obstacle remains navigating the draconian anti-money laundering and counter-terrorism financing regulations imposed by our own national treasuries, which inadvertently act as exclusionary barriers for unregistered or heavily persecuted grassroots groups. We observed that in 2025, grassroots CSOs experienced a 62 percent increase in legal fees simply to maintain baseline fiduciary compliance under these hostile legal frameworks. Moving forward, donors must cultivate risk-sharing frameworks that absorb the compliance burden at the institutional level, insulating the frontline defenders from administrative exhaustion.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
NGO Representative: I must interject to amplify Marcus's point regarding administrative exhaustion. Expecting grassroots constituencies to continuously adapt to both physical persecution and suffocating bureaucratic compliance is entirely unsustainable. To circumvent hostile state apparatuses, our strategic imperative is the cultivation of broad-based, intersectional feminist networks that cut across identities based on class, race, and gender. When feminists take to the streets, deeply rooted community work and intersectional organizing can fundamentally expand the scope of our advocacy. By linking feminist concerns to broader issues of neoliberal exploitation, plural democracy, and climate action, we generate a cohesive force capable of withstanding state-sponsored backlash. We must prioritize strategic litigation, leveraging independent supreme courts as protective shields against the most aggressive forms of anti-gender policy backsliding, while simultaneously building decentralized mutual aid networks that operate outside the purview of the state.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Audience Member 2: Building on Elena's point about strategic litigation and institutional shields, my question challenges the efficacy of these traditional democratic institutions. How do we rely on the judiciary or parliamentary mechanisms when norm-spoiling tactics and authoritarian executive overreach are actively politicizing the courts and eroding the independence of the very bodies meant to protect human rights?
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Researcher: The politicization of the judiciary you highlight is a cornerstone of the authoritarian playbook. The United Nations Research Institute for Social Development, UNRISD, emphasizes that countering gender backlash inherently requires safeguarding and strengthening the broader democratic principles and practices. We cannot treat gender equality as an isolated thematic silo; it is the vanguard of democratic resilience. When political processes become violent and exclude marginalized groups, the ability to demand equal representation and inclusive deliberation is fundamentally compromised. Controlling voices is a primary tactic, often achieved through intimidation or reprisals against those collaborating with international mechanisms. Between May 2022 and April 2023, the UN documented 140 incidents of reprisals for cooperating with the United Nations on human rights across 40 countries, affecting at least 108 women and girls. Therefore, our counter-strategy must involve transversal alliances that unite women's organizations with broader pro-democracy and anti-corruption movements to collectively defend the structural integrity of independent oversight bodies.
</p>
<break time="1.5s"/>
<p>
Youth Activist: Exactly, and this transversal approach is precisely why authentic, localized intergenerational exchange is non-negotiable for our collective survival. The opposition utilizes a global gag rule mentality, not just in literal policy, but in their cultural strategy to silence dissent and enforce compliance. To neutralize this, we must formulate communication approaches that dismantle their fabricated contradictions. Positive narratives can highlight the tangible benefits gender equality brings to wider communities, casting key terms like family or care—which are frequently monopolized by conservative actors—in an inclusive, egalitarian light. We need peer exchange mechanisms that translate youthful digital innovation into sustained, transnational solidarity. We must transition from fragmented, defensive postures into a proactive, cohesive ecosystem that refuses to cede our fundamental human rights to the proponents of an exclusionary past.
</p>
</prosody>
</speak>
